Scenario: adversarial second-hop re-handoff of the attended alarm (round-2 fix,
bin/fm-claude-stop-autoarm.sh autoarm_commit()'s own handoff builder at
lines ~375-381), driven live against the real production script with three
real concurrent Stop-hook firings sharing one session lock:

  A: attended-alarm owner (gen1), exhausts its bounded retries with the
     session block budget already over BLOCK_BUDGET, reaches
     autoarm_commit_attended_alarm(), and hands the alarm off (rc=3) because a
     live parked successor (B) exists.
  B: parked on A's gen1 claim; becomes owner of gen2 (arming) via the
     handoff. Before B's own autoarm_commit() call lands, a third firing (C)
     parks on B's still-open gen2 claim, so B's own write detects a live
     parked successor and re-hands off through its generic handoff builder
     instead of committing directly.
  C: parked on B's gen2 claim; receives the re-handoff, reads its "alarm"
     field, and (if the round-2 fix holds) commits the alarm directly as its
     own gen3 outcome.

Observed (this run):
  rc-a = 0   (A handed off silently, no stderr)
  rc-b = 0   (B re-handed off silently, no stderr -- confirms B did NOT
              commit the alarm itself, matching the fix's intended behavior)
  rc-c = 2   (C delivered the alarm as its own rewake)

  c.out:
    FIRSTMATE SUPERVISION IS GENUINELY DOWN: 1 task(s) in flight - the
    Stop-owned auto-arm exhausted its bounded retries and one failure
    notice, no live watcher or automatic continuation exists, and this
    session block budget is exhausted.
    Keep this session attended and diagnose the automatic Stop-hook and
    watcher startup before relying on unattended supervision; do not launch
    a manual background arm from this notice.

  Final ledger (state/.claude-autoarm-epoch):
    epoch=3 owner_pid=<C's pid> outcome=failed-suppressed updated_at=...

  state/.claude-autoarm-failure-alarmed: PRESENT (created by C's own commit)
  state/.claude-autoarm-parked:          ABSENT (no stray parked record)
  state/.claude-autoarm-handoff:         ABSENT (no stray handoff record)

This confirms the round-2 fix: the "alarm=1" field survives a second hop
through autoarm_commit()'s own re-handoff builder, so the once-per-episode
alarm marker is created exactly once, by the firing that actually delivers
the banner (C), even when the attended-alarm handoff itself gets superseded
a second time before it is committed. Before the round-2 fix, B's generic
handoff builder only ever emitted "notice=1" for a non-empty marker, so C
would have delivered the banner text but never created
state/.claude-autoarm-failure-alarmed, silently breaking the
once-per-episode invariant (exactly the bug round-2 fixed, which no test in
the repo had exercised through a real two-hop chain until this run).

No existing test in tests/fm-claude-stop-autoarm.test.sh drives this exact
three-firing chain; this was built and run as a standalone script against a
fresh, disposable fixture home (same install_autoarm_scripts()/
write_arm_fixture("gated-failed") pattern the existing suite uses), outside
the worktree, and torn down after this run.
